WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.480
In the last few years, there have been 

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:05.320
a lot of breathless news articles
about the upcoming shortage of chocolate.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.120
Around the world, demand for chocolate is
rising, particularly in China and India,

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.720
and the supply isn't matching it. 

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.800
It turns out that the cocoa beans
that get turned into chocolate

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.320
are a really difficult crop to grow.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.480
Cocoa needs fairly specific
climate conditions.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:19.800
It needs a lot of management. 

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.360
And about 30 to 40% of the entire world's
crop of cocoa

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:28.720
is just lost to pests
and diseases every year.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.240
So what the modern world needs is new,
hardier, more pest

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.280
resistant varieties of cocoa
with higher yields.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.360
Botanists
send plant cuttings around the world,

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.240
so they can cross
breed them and see what the results are.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.320
But the trouble is: if you're sending
cuttings around the world,

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.400
you might be sending diseases with them,
and that could be devastating.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.120
Which is why in a rural bit
of southern England, there

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.560
sits the International Cocoa
Quarantine Center.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.960
- The quarantine process
lasts for two years.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.880
The reason why it is so long
is because viruses

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.480
can potentially stay latent in the plant
for a long period of time.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.320
We generally receive plant material
from what we call

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.640
"gene banks",
so these are the research centers

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:13.720
that have collections of lots
of different types of cocoa plants.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.560
That plant material arrives
as what we call "bud sticks".

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.840
We will inspect that material
in the laboratory, make sure there's

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.400
no signs of fungal spores
or insects on it.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.320
But whilst it's quite easy to spot a pest
or a fungal problem,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.600
viral diseases can be much more difficult
to spot.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.280
- When a researcher or grower orders 

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.240
a cutting from here, they know that
they won't also get something nasty

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.040
that could infect their 

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.200
existing cocoa crop
or even their entire region's cocoa crop.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.640
But what they'll receive
isn't the original plant that was sent in.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.920
This isn't like quarantine for parcels
or for people.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.360
They don't take an incoming cocoa plant,
put it in the bit of this warehouse

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.440
that I'm not allowed to be in because it's
quarantined, and then just send it on.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:59.040
After the two years' quarantine,
the center here has a known safe plant,

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.080
one of these that I'm actually allowed
to stand near, and then they can send

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.560
cuttings out on request,
over and over and over again.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.640
- There are other quarantine centers
for other plants.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.800
There's a quarantine facility
for bananas, for example.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.800
I believe there's one in Scotland
for potatoes.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.840
A particular thing about cocoa
is that a lot of the pests

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.560
and diseases of cocoa are only found
in particular geographical regions.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:29.040
If growers can be supplied with plants
which are more resilient to these threats,

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.160
so more resilient to pests and diseases,

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:32.000
and more resilient 

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.600
to extreme temperatures, then that helps
in securing the future of the crop.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.080
- There is one obvious question, of course,
if all the unsafe cocoa

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:45.000
that might have pests in it is sitting
somewhere around here in rural England...

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.120
what if one of those pests escapes?

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.040
- In the UK, we don't grow cocoa. 

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.560
It's too cold,
but that's actually a positive thing

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.440
in terms of the location
of the quarantine center.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.240
So the fact that we're located
in a country that does not grow cocoa

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.720
means there's
no risk of pests and diseases

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.920
which are endemic to cocoa
entering the facility.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:11.120
So if we were to receive any material
which had a nasty disease,

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.040
that material would be discarded. 

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.240
It would be autoclaved
just as a precaution, but

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:19.480
the chance of it
establishing here would be almost zero.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:22.480
- If there does end up being a worldwide
cocoa shortage,

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:25.480
it doesn't mean that the grocery store
shelves will end up empty.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:28.920
It just means that the price of chocolate
will rise and rise and rise

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.360
as supply falls. 

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.840
And the big manufacturers
would very much like to make sure

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.200
that chocolate
does not go back to being a luxury.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.000
And the folks here at the quarantine
center 

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.520
are doing their bit
to make sure that doesn't happen.

